WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.060 --> 00:00:05.490
The explosive ending of Joaquin Phoenix's
origin story for the Joker will have fans

00:00:05.490 --> 00:00:09.560
debating his version of the iconic comic book
villain for years to come.

00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:13.940
However, the original ending was even more
chilling and had a brutal connection to Heath

00:00:13.940 --> 00:00:15.199
Ledger's Joker.

00:00:15.199 --> 00:00:19.700
On top of that, over 30 minutes of additional
footage didn't make it into the final film,

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:23.000
meaning there are lots of deleted scenes missing
from the theatrical cut.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:27.720
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, I'm Jan and in
this video I'm revealing all the deleted scenes

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:32.239
from 2019's Joker and explaining the alternate
ending you never got to see.

00:00:32.239 --> 00:00:36.220
There will of course be spoilers, so take
care if you haven't seen the movie yet.

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:40.610
In the film, we watch Arthur Fleck pass through
multiple breaking points on the way to his

00:00:40.610 --> 00:00:42.700
transformation into the Joker.

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:46.840
One of these is his discovery of the abuse
he suffered as a three-year-old child, but

00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:51.440
the original screenplay delved even deeper
into Arthur's troubled childhood.

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:55.700
In a scene scrapped from the final film, Arthur
revealed to Sophie that as a child he'd been

00:00:55.700 --> 00:01:00.500
bullied by other children who mocked him and
nicknamed him him "Happy" because of his affliction.

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:04.580
By the time he was ten years old, Arthur was
so sick of it that he took a razor to his

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:08.600
mouth and cut a smile onto his face, telling
his bullies: "You want happy?

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:10.650
Here, how's this for happy?"

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:15.020
The childhood injury left Arthur with scars
around the corners of his mouth and brings

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:19.020
to mind the stories of how Heath Ledger's
Joker claimed he got his scars.

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:21.729
"You wanna know how I got these scars?

00:01:21.729 --> 00:01:26.740
My father was a drinker
And one night..

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:29.590
He comes at me with the knife.

00:01:29.590 --> 00:01:30.820
'Why so serious?'

00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:32.710
He sticks the blade in my mouth.

00:01:32.710 --> 00:01:37.140
'Let's put a smile on that face.'"

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:41.820
The original ending for Joker was going to
revisit Arthur's horrific childhood self-harm

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:47.020
in a scene where after the police car crashes,
Arthur escapes, grabs a piece of broken glass

00:01:47.020 --> 00:01:51.109
and uses it to carve a long smile into the
corners of his mouth again.

00:01:51.109 --> 00:01:56.079
Although Joker is R-rated and obviously violent,
it feels like this original ending would have

00:01:56.079 --> 00:01:58.540
been even more shocking and brutal.

00:01:58.540 --> 00:02:03.109
It's possible the filmmakers or studio considered
showing Arthur inflict this injury on himself

00:02:03.109 --> 00:02:05.329
too gruesome to show on screen.

00:02:05.329 --> 00:02:09.550
There is a nod to this original idea in the
final version of the film though when, during

00:02:09.550 --> 00:02:13.980
the riots, Arthur takes the blood in his mouth
caused by the car crash and paints a bloody

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:17.140
elongated smile onto his face with his fingers.

00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:21.680
It's also possible the studio decided they
wanted to avoid any suggestion that this Joker

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:23.569
could be connected with Heath Ledger's version.

00:02:23.569 --> 00:02:29.220
"So I stick a razor in my mouth and do this
.. to myself.

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:30.849
Now I'm always smiling."

00:02:30.849 --> 00:02:35.090
The filmmakers have stressed multiple times
that the movie is not connected in any way

00:02:35.090 --> 00:02:40.640
to the DCEU, and the screenplay also states
that the "story takes place in its own universe

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:44.480
[and] has no connection to any of the DC films
that have come before it."

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:48.459
Arthur's facial scars would have also made
the scene where he meets the young Bruce Wayne

00:02:48.459 --> 00:02:53.300
even creepier because, originally, young Bruce
was going to stare at the scars around Arthur's

00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:58.240
mouth and then reach out to touch them, foreshadowing
the future connection between the two characters.

00:02:58.240 --> 00:02:59.240
"You..

00:02:59.240 --> 00:03:02.459
You complete me."

00:03:02.459 --> 00:03:07.620
The very final scene of Arthur in Arkham State
Hospital was also going to be quite different.

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:12.190
Originally, his cut smile was going to be
all stitched up and the scene with the hospital

00:03:12.190 --> 00:03:14.040
doctor played out a little longer.

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:18.129
In an earlier script, she asked him if he
was keeping up-to-date with his journal and

00:03:18.129 --> 00:03:19.920
if he'd written about his episode.

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:24.120
Arthur nodded and said it was how he remembered
it, but when the doctor looked in the book

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:25.819
it was full of empty pages.

00:03:25.819 --> 00:03:30.409
The movie then ended with an orderly walking
Arthur back to his cell while he danced to

00:03:30.409 --> 00:03:32.860
the tune of Frank Sinatra's "That's Life".

00:03:32.860 --> 00:03:37.439
In contrast to the theatrical version, in
the original screenplay, Arthur doesn't appear

00:03:37.439 --> 00:03:42.299
to kill the hospital doctor, nor does he seem
to be about to escape from the ward.

00:03:42.299 --> 00:03:46.040
On top of these changes to the ending, there
are numerous deleted scenes from the first

00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:50.340
cut of the movie which, according to director
Todd Phillips, is a solid half hour longer

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:51.430
than the final version.

00:03:51.430 --> 00:03:55.150
There are some key moments from the trailers
such as these which didn't make it into the

00:03:55.150 --> 00:03:59.540
final movie and I'm going to go through the
screenplay and footage from the set to tell

00:03:59.540 --> 00:04:01.530
you what these deleted scenes are.

00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:06.709
Arthur's relationship with Sophie is imaginary
in the final version of the movie, but originally

00:04:06.709 --> 00:04:11.500
that wasn't the case in the script and all
of Arthur and Sophie's scenes were real and

00:04:11.500 --> 00:04:13.050
did actually happen.

00:04:13.050 --> 00:04:16.989
There are also several additional scenes between
Arthur and Sophie which were deleted from

00:04:16.989 --> 00:04:18.000
the final film.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:22.790
After Arthur stalks and follows Sophie to
where she works at Gotham Savings Bank, there's

00:04:22.790 --> 00:04:27.350
on set footage of Joaquin Phoenix coming out
of the bank meaning there was an additional

00:04:27.350 --> 00:04:28.500
scene inside.

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:32.930
In the screenplay, Arthur went inside the
bank and walked up to Sophie who worked there

00:04:32.930 --> 00:04:36.210
as a teller, and pretended he wanted to open
an account.

00:04:36.210 --> 00:04:40.720
In this sequence which was scrapped from the
movie, Arthur saw Sophie visibly shudder when

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:43.699
an older bank manager put his hand on her
shoulder.

00:04:43.699 --> 00:04:48.100
Later, when Arthur and Sophie are out together,
she tells him she's been fired after filing

00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:52.170
a complaint about the manager harassing her
and trying to sleep with her.

00:04:52.170 --> 00:04:56.570
And there's a deleted scene in the trailers
where Arthur is carrying his wand of flowers

00:04:56.570 --> 00:04:59.490
as he walks along the corridor of his apartment
block.

00:04:59.490 --> 00:05:03.699
He's wearing his full Joker outfit and is
on his way to the Murray Franklin show.

00:05:03.699 --> 00:05:07.650
In the original screenplay, Arthur leaves
the flowers and an envelope full of money

00:05:07.650 --> 00:05:12.470
at Sophie's apartment door, together with
a note asking her to watch him on TV.

00:05:12.470 --> 00:05:16.440
This scene changed in the final film with
Arthur simply exiting his building without

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:18.030
taking anything to Sophie.

00:05:18.030 --> 00:05:22.380
There's an interesting deleted scene after
Arthur is sacked and returns to the talent

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:24.840
agency to collect his things from his locker.

00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:29.410
After Arthur leaves, there's on-set footage
of Randall chasing after him and the two of

00:05:29.410 --> 00:05:34.550
them arguing, then Arthur taking off Randall's
clown nose and throwing it into the road.

00:05:34.550 --> 00:05:39.199
The original screenplay explains that during
this cut scene, Randall is annoyed that Arthur

00:05:39.199 --> 00:05:41.509
mentioned that he'd given him the gun.

00:05:41.509 --> 00:05:45.710
Randall warns Arthur that the cops are all
over the subway murders, are now looking for

00:05:45.710 --> 00:05:48.990
a clown and that they'll eventually track
him down.

00:05:48.990 --> 00:05:53.270
Arthur denies he had anything to do with the
killings, but Randall seems to know the truth

00:05:53.270 --> 00:05:57.169
and he goes on to tell Arthur that a lot of
people in the city are happy about what happened

00:05:57.169 --> 00:05:59.090
to the Wall Street guys.

00:05:59.090 --> 00:06:03.900
Randall also tells his ex-colleague that masks
are now being sold based on Arthur's clown

00:06:03.900 --> 00:06:04.900
face.

00:06:04.900 --> 00:06:09.759
A cut scene teased in the trailers shows Arthur
getting thrown out of Wayne Hall after his

00:06:09.759 --> 00:06:11.500
run-in with Thomas Wayne.

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:16.319
The screenplay describes the scene in more
detail and that Arthur, as a performer, "knows

00:06:16.319 --> 00:06:20.000
how to take a fall [and] plays it up in front
of [the protestors] for all it's worth.

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:23.990
He rolls to his feet with a bit of panache
[and the] protestors go crazy, cheering and

00:06:23.990 --> 00:06:27.580
applauding his act [with Arthur taking] a
deep dramatic bow."

00:06:27.580 --> 00:06:32.280
This deleted scene together with Randall's
scrapped scene definitely play up even more

00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:37.639
the sense of how Arthur's actions are igniting
Gotham's citizens to rise up against the rich.

00:06:37.639 --> 00:06:42.750
A really interesting fantasy subplot that
was cut from the final film involves Arthur's

00:06:42.750 --> 00:06:44.129
ginger cat.

00:06:44.129 --> 00:06:48.400
In the original screenplay, the cat appeared
to Arthur whenever he was in his apartment,

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:52.830
purring or staring at him and generally observing
whatever he was doing.

00:06:52.830 --> 00:06:57.419
And as Arthur slowly transformed into the
Joker, he began talking to the cat.

00:06:57.419 --> 00:07:01.449
The final scene with the cat happens just
after Arthur kills Randall.

00:07:01.449 --> 00:07:05.360
We see it sitting on a window sill though
the script makes a point of saying that it

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:10.659
has no reflection, suggesting that all along
the cat was just another manifestation of

00:07:10.659 --> 00:07:13.510
Arthur's delusions and lack of grip on reality.

00:07:13.510 --> 00:07:18.410
At that moment, he lets the cat out of the
window, telling it to go be free, indicating

00:07:18.410 --> 00:07:24.340
how Arthur was finally setting himself free
to embrace the personality of the Joker entirely.

00:07:24.340 --> 00:07:28.950
This imaginary cat could have been an intriguing
subplot that paralleled Arthur's descent into

00:07:28.950 --> 00:07:33.370
madness, but instead it looks like in the
final movie, the filmmakers took the fantasy

00:07:33.370 --> 00:07:37.600
element of the cat and merged it with Sophie's
storyline instead.

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:42.300
The scene after Arthur kills the three Wall
Street guys on the subway was originally quite

00:07:42.300 --> 00:07:43.300
different.

00:07:43.300 --> 00:07:47.360
In the original screenplay, Arthur runs into
the Public Bathroom and ends up trying to

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:51.800
shoot himself in the head before hiding the
gun away, then washing the clown make-up off

00:07:51.800 --> 00:07:52.800
his face.

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:57.409
In the final film, this scene was completely
changed, with Arthur dancing around and seemingly

00:07:57.409 --> 00:07:59.669
embracing this side of himself.

00:07:59.669 --> 00:08:03.930
In fact, Arthur's dancing was something Phillips
and Phoenix gave more prominence to in the

00:08:03.930 --> 00:08:05.370
final version.

00:08:05.370 --> 00:08:09.460
Phillips said in a Q&amp;A that he always thought
"that Arthur had music in him" and that they

00:08:09.460 --> 00:08:13.639
added the dancing to the bathroom scene to
"show that [the music is] fighting to get

00:08:13.639 --> 00:08:15.030
out."

00:08:15.030 --> 00:08:19.460
Another deleted scene had Arthur personally
going to Thomas Wayne's offices to hand-deliver

00:08:19.460 --> 00:08:21.760
a letter from his mother to her former employer.

00:08:21.760 --> 00:08:26.490
There's on-set footage of a scene we never
saw in the final film of Joaquin Phoenix outside

00:08:26.490 --> 00:08:30.470
an office and running up to someone with a
letter before falling over.

00:08:30.470 --> 00:08:35.640
In the screenplay, Arthur also goes inside
the office and asks to see Thomas Wayne.

00:08:35.640 --> 00:08:40.700
But the receptionist refuses and he ends up
getting thrown out of the building by security

00:08:40.700 --> 00:08:41.700
guards.

00:08:41.700 --> 00:08:45.670
After Arthur's mother is taken to hospital,
there's a deleted scene in the screenplay

00:08:45.670 --> 00:08:49.560
where a doctor and couple of nurses come up
to Arthur and ask him if he's really the guy

00:08:49.560 --> 00:08:52.070
from the clip that was played on the Murray
Franklin show.

00:08:52.070 --> 00:08:57.740
"When I was a little boy and told people I
was going to be a comedian, everyone laughed

00:08:57.740 --> 00:08:58.740
at me.

00:08:58.740 --> 00:09:00.140
Well noone's laughing now."

00:09:00.140 --> 00:09:02.700
"You can say that again pal."

00:09:02.700 --> 00:09:06.829
Arthur tries to deny it, but the doctor and
nurses are convinced he's the guy they saw

00:09:06.829 --> 00:09:11.700
on TV and they begin to point out how unfunny
he was and that Murray made a fool out of

00:09:11.700 --> 00:09:12.700
him.

00:09:12.700 --> 00:09:16.530
Arthur ends up really mad and tells them to
go before he strangles all three of them with

00:09:16.530 --> 00:09:17.649
a stethoscope.

00:09:17.649 --> 00:09:22.010
Later, after Arthur has killed his mother,
there's a deleted scene from the trailers

00:09:22.010 --> 00:09:24.390
which shows Arthur alone outside.

00:09:24.390 --> 00:09:28.480
This looks like the scene from the screenplay
where Arthur is at his mother's grave in Gotham's

00:09:28.480 --> 00:09:30.100
Potter's Field Cemetery.

00:09:30.100 --> 00:09:34.610
Arthur was the only person who attended his
mother's funeral and the two detectives tracking

00:09:34.610 --> 00:09:37.100
Arthur were also watching him from a distance
at that point.

00:09:37.100 --> 00:09:38.100
Another curious bit of deleted footage from
the trailers has Arthur walking down the corridor

00:09:38.100 --> 00:09:39.100
with a set of flowers.

00:09:39.100 --> 00:09:40.100
This is when he's on his way to Murray Franklin's
show as he's got the full Joker outfit on,

00:09:40.100 --> 00:09:41.100
but for the same scene in the final film Arthur
doesn't have the flowers.

00:09:41.100 --> 00:09:42.750
One of Joaquin Phoenix's favourite scenes
ended up cut from the final edit.

00:09:42.750 --> 00:09:46.741
The scene was part of the rant and monologue
that Joker delivers on the Murray Franklin

00:09:46.741 --> 00:09:49.670
show just before he shoots the host in the
head.

00:09:49.670 --> 00:09:53.580
In an interview with The Associated Press,
Phoenix explained that he experimented a lot

00:09:53.580 --> 00:09:57.779
with his performance, how in some scenes he
would cry and in other versions of the same

00:09:57.779 --> 00:10:00.079
scene he would make jokes or be angry.

00:10:00.079 --> 00:10:04.090
The actor claimed that his best take for the
rant Arthur gives on Murray's show couldn't

00:10:04.090 --> 00:10:08.180
be used because it didn't work when it was
put together with everything else in the movie.

00:10:08.180 --> 00:10:12.740
According to Phoenix, the version used in
the movie isn't very good, but it worked best

00:10:12.740 --> 00:10:14.830
in the context of the entire film.

00:10:14.830 --> 00:10:18.090
So, what did you think of this Joker origin
story?

00:10:18.090 --> 00:10:20.920
And are there any deleted scenes you wish
had been included?

